  "notes_for_editor": "NRHP nomination (06000864) is not digitised on NPGallery, so no nomination was read; the 2006 listing year comes from our record only and is not in the story. USCG Historian page (archived) was not reachable. Tribune 1895 OCR columns are jumbled: the attribution of the 'mistook' remark to the Nellie Smith's captain is inferred from the fragments ('Captain Thomas ... of the Smith, said'); a human should check the page image. 1918 Bulletin OCR is poor; the 'no perceptible damage' quote is OCR as-is. The 1925 Bulletin rescue of three men on 7 September is dated only by its volume year. Carlow: USLHS roster says to 1902; Tribune 18 Oct 1902 says he retired about two years earlier owing to ill health and died by suicide at Fort Tompkins; LighthouseFriends says he was sent to the Marine Hospital in 1902 and died after a visit in October 1902. Epkens quote is second-hand via LighthouseFriends (original reporter/paper not given). Roster: Hosea F. Geer, John Gonze appear in Bulletins (John Gouze/Gonze) as 2nd assistant (roster has John O. Ganze); Bulletin 1927 calls Connolly first assistant, consistent with roster. LighthouseFriends also lists Samuel Jeffries (1917) and Charles E. Jones Jr. (-1920), not in USLHS. Height: USLHS 35 ft base to lantern centre; LighthouseFriends calls it a fifty-one-foot tower (probably focal height). Lens status and fate after 1950 not established. Wikipedia says Fresnel lens removed 1950 (unverified). Story mentions the 1896-97 Bulletin volume label because fiscal year is not confirmed. Fact-check 2026-10-27: Bulletin 1927 rescue text is on PDF p.35, commendation list on p.44 (footnote now cites both). 4-5 Oct 1918 explosion: source says 'on October 4 and 5', not 'night'. The 1918 'vibrated and shook considerably' quote is reconstructed from poor OCR.",
